  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on wet-bulb temperature as the primary metric for heat stress, which may not capture the full suite of human thermal comfort variables.",
      "what": "This study analyzed 251 rapidly developing Chinese cities, categorized into four background climate zones, to measure how three-dimensional urban form influences the coupled cooling and humidifying effects of vegetation transpiration on wet-bulb temperature. The analysis revealed that in high-rise compact urban forms, transpiration raises wet-bulb temperatures by increasing local humidity without providing significant temperature cooling, resulting in a population-weighted exposure intensity 23 times higher than in low-rise dispersed forms.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that urban greening in hot, humid climates can counterproductively increase human heat stress depending on the surrounding physical architecture, challenging the universal assumption that urban vegetation always mitigates heat risk."
    }
